{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "כי ירחק", :translit "ki yirchak", :gloss "when [the place] is far"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Verse begins and ends with the letter kaf"}], :frame {:text "After slaughter, a shochet must examine the lung by inserting his hand to check for lesions that would render the animal treifah. This examination is a required part of the slaughter process even when one has already performed the slaughter (Chullin 9a). The Ba'al HaTurim derives this requirement from the kaf at both ends of the verse.", :sources ["Chullin 9a"]}} {:quotes [{:he "כאשר צויתיך", :translit "ka'asher tziviticha", :gloss "as I have commanded you"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "כאשר צויתיך", :b "רוב אחד בעוף ורוב שנים בבהמה"}], :frame {:text "The phrase 'as I have commanded you' in Deuteronomy 12:21 is read by the Sages as referring to the laws of slaughter that were given orally to Moses at Sinai (Chullin 28a). Among these laws is the principle that severing the majority of one siman (trachea or esophagus) suffices for valid slaughter of fowl, while the majority of both simanim is required for animals (Chullin 28a).", :sources ["Chullin 28a"]}} {:quotes [{:he "כאשר", :translit "ka'asher", :gloss "as"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :notarikon, :word "כאשר", :expansion ["כולו" "של" "רובו" "אחד"]}], :frame {:text "The principle that the majority of an organ is treated as the whole is applied in the laws of slaughter: severing the majority of the required simanim accomplishes what severing the entirety would (Chullin 28a). The Ba'al HaTurim reads this principle as encoded in the letters of the word כאשר itself.", :sources ["Chullin 28a"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Verse begins and ends with the letter kaf", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :gematria, :a "כאשר צויתיך", :a-value 1057, :b "רוב אחד בעוף ורוב שנים בבהמה", :b-value 1047, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? false} {:kind :notarikon, :word "כאשר", :expansion ["כולו" "של" "